1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,270
In 1958, oil prospectors flying over the
Libyan Desert saw something that had no

2
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:09,430
business being there.

3
00:00:09,990 --> 00:00:12,030
An American bomber.

4
00:00:12,533 --> 00:00:17,290
A B-24 Liberator, lying on the open sand,

5
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:21,630
some four hundred and forty
miles southeast of Benghazi.

6
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:24,630
And no one aboard.

7
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:30,277
The name painted on its
nose was Lady Be Good.

8
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:32,380
According to the U.S.

9
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:37,390
Army Air Forces, it had
disappeared fifteen years earlier,

10
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:44,830
on its very first combat mission: a raid
on the harbor of Naples, in April 1943.

11
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:48,910
Every other bomber from
that raid came home.

12
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:53,870
This one flew past its own
base, deep into the desert,

13
00:00:54,020 --> 00:00:58,530
until the fuel ran out and nine
young men jumped into the dark.

14
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:04,530
What happened to them next was
written down, in a small diary,

15
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:06,850
by one of the men who walked.

16
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:13,690
[long pause] This is their
story, told from the record.

17
00:01:18,950 --> 00:01:24,180
To understand how a bomber ends up
four hundred miles inside the Sahara,

18
00:01:24,890 --> 00:01:28,160
we have to start at a place called Soluch.

19
00:01:29,230 --> 00:01:35,580
In the spring of 1943, Soluch
was a desert airstrip in Libya,

20
00:01:36,050 --> 00:01:39,560
about thirty-four miles southeast
of the port of Benghazi.

21
00:01:40,750 --> 00:01:42,780
From fields like this one,

22
00:01:43,190 --> 00:01:48,320
American heavy bombers flew north across
the Mediterranean to strike targets in

23
00:01:48,370 --> 00:01:49,370
Italy.

24
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:54,840
The aircraft were B-24
Liberators: four engines,

25
00:01:55,270 --> 00:01:58,780
and enough range to cross
the sea and come back.

26
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:04,180
Soluch was home to the 376th Bomb Group.

27
00:02:05,350 --> 00:02:09,640
And in late March, the group
received a brand-new aircraft,

28
00:02:10,110 --> 00:02:14,245
serial number forty-one,
twenty-four three-oh-one.

29
00:02:15,090 --> 00:02:18,240
Its crew would call it Lady Be Good.

30
00:02:19,620 --> 00:02:23,330
The men assigned to fly
her were just as new.

31
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:28,510
The pilot was First
Lieutenant William Hatton.

32
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:33,050
His co-pilot, Second
Lieutenant Robert Toner.

33
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:37,450
The navigator was Second Lieutenant D.P.

34
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:44,520
Hays, and the bombardier,
Second Lieutenant John Woravka.

35
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:51,070
Behind them sat the enlisted men:
Technical Sergeant Harold Ripslinger,

36
00:02:51,540 --> 00:02:52,770
the flight engineer.

37
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:57,630
Technical Sergeant Robert
LaMotte, the radio operator.

38
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:05,070
And three staff sergeants, Guy Shelley,
Vernon Moore and Samuel Adams,

39
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:08,910
who manned the guns and helped
with the engines and the radio.

40
00:03:10,940 --> 00:03:11,940
Nine men.

41
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:16,230
They had arrived in Libya
on the eighteenth of March.

42
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:20,390
They had never flown a
combat mission together.

43
00:03:22,180 --> 00:03:23,790
Remember these names.

44
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:30,490
Because the desert would keep some of them
for seventeen years, and one of them,

45
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:32,530
it has never given back.

46
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:39,390
Lady Be Good had been with the group for
less than two weeks when the orders came.

47
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:48,130
Tomorrow, the fourth of April, the 376th
would send its Liberators against Naples.

48
00:03:49,340 --> 00:03:52,670
For the new crew, it would
be the first mission.

49
00:03:54,340 --> 00:03:59,790
For the aircraft, it would be the
first, the last, and the only one.

50
00:04:04,970 --> 00:04:08,155
On the afternoon of April the fourth,

51
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:13,080
twenty-five B-24s were assigned
to bomb the harbor of Naples.

52
00:04:14,290 --> 00:04:20,400
They would go in two waves: twelve
aircraft first, then thirteen more.

53
00:04:21,750 --> 00:04:24,440
Lady Be Good was in the second wave,

54
00:04:24,490 --> 00:04:30,120
and among the last planes off the ground
at Soluch, at around a quarter past two.

55
00:04:31,430 --> 00:04:34,040
The plan was simple on paper.

56
00:04:35,090 --> 00:04:39,760
Climb out over the Mediterranean,
fly north for hours,

57
00:04:40,190 --> 00:04:44,780
bomb the harbor in the evening, and
come home across the sea in the dark.

58
00:04:46,210 --> 00:04:50,360
The desert, though, had its
own plans that afternoon.

59
00:04:51,690 --> 00:04:55,240
Strong winds had whipped
the sand into the air.

60
00:04:56,290 --> 00:04:57,620
Visibility collapsed.

61
00:04:59,410 --> 00:05:05,820
In the second wave, many of the bombers
gave up and turned back to Soluch.

62
00:05:06,510 --> 00:05:09,520
Lady Be Good was not one of them.

63
00:05:11,050 --> 00:05:16,620
For a crew on its first mission, turning
back would have been easy to explain.

64
00:05:17,490 --> 00:05:18,700
They didn't.

65
00:05:19,730 --> 00:05:26,900
They kept flying north, over open water,
toward a target none of them had ever seen.

66
00:05:28,090 --> 00:05:33,400
What happened over Italy is where the
record starts to disagree with itself.

67
00:05:35,410 --> 00:05:38,660
One account, published by the U.S.

68
00:05:39,250 --> 00:05:44,380
National Archives, says Lady Be Good
never caught up with the formation,

69
00:05:44,430 --> 00:05:48,200
and turned around just
before reaching the target.

70
00:05:49,210 --> 00:05:54,080
Another, based on the group's records,
says she did join the formation,

71
00:05:54,130 --> 00:05:57,480
but that poor visibility
hid the target below.

72
00:05:58,750 --> 00:06:02,440
Either way, the result was the same.

73
00:06:03,630 --> 00:06:06,800
Lady Be Good did not bomb Naples.

74
00:06:08,350 --> 00:06:12,680
And by now, she was flying
home on her own, in the dark.

75
00:06:13,880 --> 00:06:19,070
The flight back to Libya was long,
and almost all of it over water.

76
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:21,350
No lights below.

77
00:06:22,360 --> 00:06:23,750
No horizon.

78
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:27,910
Just the instruments, the engines,

79
00:06:28,260 --> 00:06:31,970
and nine men waiting for the
coast of Africa to appear.

80
00:06:33,740 --> 00:06:38,430
Then, shortly after midnight,
a voice came over the radio.

81
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:41,790
It was William Hatton.

82
00:06:42,980 --> 00:06:44,170
And he had a problem.

83
00:06:49,340 --> 00:06:53,690
At twelve minutes past midnight,
according to the group's records,

84
00:06:54,260 --> 00:06:56,050
William Hatton called the base.

85
00:06:57,960 --> 00:07:00,670
His automatic direction finder,

86
00:07:00,840 --> 00:07:05,810
the instrument that should have
pointed the way home, was not working.

87
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:09,410
He needed to know where the base was.

88
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:15,770
It is worth pausing on how little
the crew had to work with.

89
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:20,090
Below them was darkness.

90
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:27,270
If there was cloud, there was no moon
to steer by and no coastline to see.

91
00:07:27,750 --> 00:07:33,390
A navigator over water at night had
his charts, his watch, his compass,

92
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:36,030
and whatever the radio could tell him.

93
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:40,170
So the radio was everything.

94
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:46,690
And the answer that came back that night
would shape every hour that followed.

95
00:07:47,870 --> 00:07:54,180
The official report, written years later
by the team sent to recover the crew,

96
00:07:54,870 --> 00:07:57,080
reconstructs what happened next.

97
00:07:59,030 --> 00:08:04,060
Lady Be Good was flying a course
of one hundred and fifty degrees,

98
00:08:04,470 --> 00:08:09,720
heading south-southeast, toward the
airfield at Benina, near Benghazi.

99
00:08:10,830 --> 00:08:16,860
The crew asked the radio direction-finding
station at Benina for a bearing.

100
00:08:17,950 --> 00:08:22,740
The station gave them one: three
hundred and thirty degrees.

101
00:08:23,490 --> 00:08:29,720
On paper, that number told them
exactly what they hoped to hear.

102
00:08:31,190 --> 00:08:35,360
It said the bomber was out to the
north-northwest of the station,

103
00:08:35,650 --> 00:08:40,560
still over the sea, still on the
right line, still coming in.

104
00:08:42,710 --> 00:08:44,620
So they kept flying.

105
00:08:45,850 --> 00:08:47,660
Here is the problem.

106
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:54,040
A simple loop antenna can tell
you the line a signal sits on.

107
00:08:55,130 --> 00:08:59,220
It cannot always tell you which
end of that line you are on.

108
00:08:59,366 --> 00:09:04,480
Three hundred and thirty degrees, and
its opposite, one hundred and fifty,

109
00:09:04,530 --> 00:09:08,080
are the same line drawn
through the station.

110
00:09:10,130 --> 00:09:14,960
The investigators concluded that the navigator
had most likely taken what is called

111
00:09:15,010 --> 00:09:16,740
a reciprocal reading.

112
00:09:17,410 --> 00:09:21,860
The bomber was not north of
Benina, out over the sea.

113
00:09:23,030 --> 00:09:27,540
It was already south of it,
over the land, flying away.

114
00:09:29,030 --> 00:09:30,980
The bearing was right.

115
00:09:32,210 --> 00:09:34,220
The direction was backwards.

116
00:09:35,530 --> 00:09:40,460
And every minute on that heading carried
nine men further from the coast they

117
00:09:40,510 --> 00:09:42,140
thought was still ahead of them.

118
00:09:43,260 --> 00:09:48,030
There is one more detail in the
record, and it is hard to read.

119
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:54,390
One account says that as the bomber
passed over the area of its base,

120
00:09:55,200 --> 00:09:58,710
flares were fired into the sky
to catch the crew's attention.

121
00:09:59,760 --> 00:10:01,910
No one aboard saw them.

122
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:07,370
Lady Be Good crossed the coast
it had been searching for,

123
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:12,250
passed over the airfield it had been
trying to reach, and flew on, south,

124
00:10:12,300 --> 00:10:14,290
into the Libyan Desert.

125
00:10:16,060 --> 00:10:20,590
For about two more hours, the
four engines kept turning.

126
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:25,690
Behind the crew, the lights of home.

127
00:10:26,230 --> 00:10:29,170
Ahead of them, nothing at all.

128
00:10:34,350 --> 00:10:38,080
Around two in the morning,
the fuel ran low.

129
00:10:39,970 --> 00:10:42,960
The engines did not all stop at once.

130
00:10:43,970 --> 00:10:46,280
When searchers later reached the wreck,

131
00:10:46,710 --> 00:10:50,400
they found that three of the four
propellers had been feathered,

132
00:10:50,710 --> 00:10:52,600
turned edge-on to the wind,

133
00:10:52,990 --> 00:10:57,480
the way a crew shuts down an engine
that has nothing left to burn.

134
00:10:59,730 --> 00:11:01,880
Hatton could not see the ground.

135
00:11:02,450 --> 00:11:09,360
As far as he knew, he was somewhere over
the Mediterranean, or just off the coast.

136
00:11:10,690 --> 00:11:13,620
Landing in the dark was not an option.

137
00:11:15,490 --> 00:11:22,360
So he gave the order every bomber crew
trained for and hoped never to hear.

138
00:11:23,890 --> 00:11:24,920
Bail out.

139
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:28,610
Nine men jumped into the night.

140
00:11:30,140 --> 00:11:32,450
They did not land in the sea.

141
00:11:33,420 --> 00:11:37,830
They landed on hard, empty ground
in the middle of the Libyan Desert.

142
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:42,990
And without them, Lady Be Good flew on.

143
00:11:44,140 --> 00:11:48,810
The empty bomber kept going for
roughly sixteen more miles,

144
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:55,490
then came down on its own, belly first,
on a flat plain of gravel, almost level,

145
00:11:55,540 --> 00:11:58,170
as if someone had landed it.

146
00:11:59,540 --> 00:12:06,090
On the ground, in total darkness, the
crew began trying to find one another.

147
00:12:07,180 --> 00:12:08,910
They fired their pistols.

148
00:12:10,040 --> 00:12:11,990
They fired signal flares.

149
00:12:13,080 --> 00:12:15,950
One by one, voices answered.

150
00:12:17,480 --> 00:12:19,170
Eight men came together.

151
00:12:19,220 --> 00:12:24,990
The ninth, the bombardier,
John Woravka, never answered.

152
00:12:26,210 --> 00:12:30,680
Robert Toner, the co-pilot,
carried a small diary.

153
00:12:31,670 --> 00:12:36,920
In it, he wrote down what had
happened, in a few plain words.

154
00:12:37,690 --> 00:12:40,800
Got lost returning.

155
00:12:41,330 --> 00:12:42,740
Out of gas.

156
00:12:43,890 --> 00:12:44,890
Jumped.

157
00:12:46,290 --> 00:12:48,640
And then: can't find John.

158
00:12:49,810 --> 00:12:51,460
All others present.

159
00:12:53,150 --> 00:12:55,500
Those were not dramatic words.

160
00:12:56,210 --> 00:13:00,040
They were the words of a young
officer trying to keep a record,

161
00:13:00,090 --> 00:13:03,980
because keeping a record was
what he had been trained to do.

162
00:13:05,120 --> 00:13:10,770
When the sun came up, the eight men
could finally see where they were.

163
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:13,410
Sand.

164
00:13:13,460 --> 00:13:14,460
Gravel.

165
00:13:14,810 --> 00:13:17,930
A flat horizon in every direction.

166
00:13:18,960 --> 00:13:20,030
No sea.

167
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:22,350
No coastline.

168
00:13:23,340 --> 00:13:29,270
No wreck, because the plane had
come down miles away, out of sight.

169
00:13:31,370 --> 00:13:34,210
They still believed the
coast could not be far.

170
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:39,430
So they made the decision that would
define everything that followed.

171
00:13:40,280 --> 00:13:46,110
They would walk, north-west, toward where
they thought the Mediterranean had to be.

172
00:13:47,610 --> 00:13:49,730
They had very little food.

173
00:13:50,580 --> 00:13:53,990
As for water, the records do not agree.

174
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,190
One says half a canteen.

175
00:13:57,900 --> 00:14:00,250
Another says a single pint.

176
00:14:01,300 --> 00:14:03,670
Another, one canteen to share.

177
00:14:05,180 --> 00:14:09,210
Whichever is true, it was not enough.

178
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:11,750
And they started walking.

179
00:14:17,050 --> 00:14:22,480
For the first days, the eight men walked
north-west across the open desert.

180
00:14:24,230 --> 00:14:30,360
They had very little food, a few silk
survival maps, and the nights were brutal.

181
00:14:31,350 --> 00:14:37,000
In one entry, Toner noted that the nights
were about thirty-five degrees Fahrenheit.

182
00:14:38,030 --> 00:14:43,220
Men who had dressed for a flight over the
sea were now sleeping on bare ground,

183
00:14:43,450 --> 00:14:44,680
close to freezing.

184
00:14:46,130 --> 00:14:50,740
As they walked, they left
things behind, on purpose.

185
00:14:52,070 --> 00:14:53,520
Pieces of parachute.

186
00:14:54,230 --> 00:14:55,230
Boots.

187
00:14:56,550 --> 00:14:58,780
Cut lengths of parachute cord.

188
00:15:00,090 --> 00:15:04,780
Some of the parachute cloth they cut
and laid out on the sand as arrows,

189
00:15:04,980 --> 00:15:08,960
weighted down with stones,
pointing the way they had gone.

190
00:15:10,390 --> 00:15:13,880
They were leaving a trail
for whoever came looking.

191
00:15:14,930 --> 00:15:19,480
They had no way of knowing that no
one would come for sixteen years.

192
00:15:20,790 --> 00:15:22,160
Why north-west?

193
00:15:24,170 --> 00:15:28,340
Because, as far as the crew
knew, the coast was close.

194
00:15:29,910 --> 00:15:34,860
They believed they had just come in from
the Mediterranean, and that the sea,

195
00:15:34,910 --> 00:15:41,420
and the British and American bases along
it, were only a short walk behind them.

196
00:15:42,330 --> 00:15:46,440
Walk north, and sooner or
later you reach the shore.

197
00:15:48,350 --> 00:15:53,200
It was the same mistake as the
night before, now made on foot.

198
00:15:53,950 --> 00:15:56,740
The bomber had not just crossed the coast.

199
00:15:57,950 --> 00:16:02,380
It had flown on for
hours, deep into Libya.

200
00:16:03,230 --> 00:16:08,400
The wreck would later be found some four
hundred and forty miles south-east of

201
00:16:08,450 --> 00:16:09,450
Benghazi.

202
00:16:11,050 --> 00:16:15,640
So every mile the men walked was
a mile in the right direction,

203
00:16:15,750 --> 00:16:19,360
toward a coast that was
simply much too far away.

204
00:16:20,490 --> 00:16:22,640
Toner kept writing as they went.

205
00:16:23,670 --> 00:16:27,760
One short entry says only: still going.

206
00:16:28,910 --> 00:16:30,920
Then the ground changed.

207
00:16:32,910 --> 00:16:37,960
After about five days, the
flat gravel gave way to dunes.

208
00:16:39,050 --> 00:16:43,060
They had walked into the edge
of the Calanscio Sand Sea,

209
00:16:43,450 --> 00:16:48,400
a vast field of sand ridges where
every step sinks and slides back.

210
00:16:50,490 --> 00:16:55,320
Toner's entry for that day, as quoted
by Air and Space Forces Magazine,

211
00:16:55,710 --> 00:17:00,660
is short: good wind, but
continuous blowing of sand.

212
00:17:02,350 --> 00:17:07,440
For men with almost no water, the
dunes were not just a slower road.

213
00:17:08,370 --> 00:17:09,620
They were a wall.

214
00:17:10,930 --> 00:17:16,800
Climbing them cost sweat, and sweat was the
one thing they could not afford to lose.

215
00:17:19,130 --> 00:17:24,200
By Friday, the ninth of April, the
group could not go on together.

216
00:17:26,170 --> 00:17:31,160
Toner wrote it down in a
single line: Shelley, Rip,

217
00:17:31,870 --> 00:17:35,060
Moore separate and try to go for help.

218
00:17:37,010 --> 00:17:42,700
Guy Shelley, Harold Ripslinger and Vernon
Moore were the three who still had the

219
00:17:42,750 --> 00:17:44,340
strength to keep moving.

220
00:17:45,190 --> 00:17:53,280
The other five, Hatton, Toner, Hays,
LaMotte and Adams, stayed where they were,

221
00:17:53,330 --> 00:17:54,600
to wait.

222
00:17:56,410 --> 00:18:00,540
In the same entry, Toner
noted something else.

223
00:18:01,550 --> 00:18:03,740
They had one parachute left.

224
00:18:04,710 --> 00:18:09,800
It was their only shelter from the
sun by day and the cold by night.

225
00:18:11,330 --> 00:18:18,040
By then, together, the eight men had
covered close to eighty miles on foot.

226
00:18:19,490 --> 00:18:23,480
The records put it somewhere between
seventy-eight and eighty-five.

227
00:18:25,230 --> 00:18:29,620
The five who stayed behind kept
waiting, and Toner kept writing.

228
00:18:31,430 --> 00:18:37,280
The next day, his entry reads: still
having prayer meetings for help.

229
00:18:38,930 --> 00:18:40,800
There was no help.

230
00:18:41,630 --> 00:18:43,720
No aircraft overhead.

231
00:18:44,770 --> 00:18:47,020
No trucks on the horizon.

232
00:18:47,770 --> 00:18:50,960
As far as the Army Air Forces knew,

233
00:18:51,530 --> 00:18:54,840
Lady Be Good had gone down
somewhere in the Mediterranean.

234
00:18:56,930 --> 00:19:02,360
The last entry in the diary is
dated Monday, the twelfth of April,

235
00:19:03,450 --> 00:19:05,420
eight days after the mission.

236
00:19:06,530 --> 00:19:11,280
Toner wrote: no help yet, very cold night.

237
00:19:12,197 --> 00:19:17,940
[long pause] After that,
the pages are blank.

238
00:19:19,210 --> 00:19:22,880
It is worth pausing on what that diary is.

239
00:19:24,970 --> 00:19:29,120
It is not a memoir, written
years later by a survivor.

240
00:19:30,310 --> 00:19:32,540
No one in this story survived.

241
00:19:33,810 --> 00:19:37,100
It is a handful of short, dated lines,

242
00:19:37,410 --> 00:19:39,680
written in pencil-thin
moments of strength,

243
00:19:39,730 --> 00:19:43,640
by a co-pilot who did not
know how his story would end.

244
00:19:44,870 --> 00:19:47,940
Eight days in the desert, in a few pages.

245
00:19:49,630 --> 00:19:56,820
Every quote you have heard in this chapter
comes from it, as published by the U.S.

246
00:19:57,510 --> 00:20:02,160
National Archives and by Air
and Space Forces Magazine.

247
00:20:03,270 --> 00:20:05,820
We have not added words to it.

248
00:20:07,310 --> 00:20:11,820
And we only know any of this
because, seventeen years later,

249
00:20:12,110 --> 00:20:16,880
someone found the diary lying beside the
men who wrote in it, and carried it home.

250
00:20:17,930 --> 00:20:20,100
And the three who went on?

251
00:20:21,100 --> 00:20:26,860
Shelley, Ripslinger and Moore kept
walking north-west, into the dunes,

252
00:20:27,030 --> 00:20:29,940
for days, after the others had stopped.

253
00:20:30,910 --> 00:20:34,760
Ripslinger, too, was
carrying a small diary.

254
00:20:36,730 --> 00:20:40,660
How far they got, and
how long they lasted,

255
00:20:40,950 --> 00:20:45,720
is something the desert would keep
to itself for a very long time.

256
00:20:47,030 --> 00:20:51,960
Because the next people to see any trace
of the Lady Be Good crew would not arrive

257
00:20:52,010 --> 00:20:53,580
in days, or weeks.

258
00:20:54,990 --> 00:20:59,480
They would arrive in a different
decade, in a different world,

259
00:20:59,790 --> 00:21:01,980
looking for something else entirely.

260
00:21:03,750 --> 00:21:05,840
They were looking for oil.

261
00:21:11,320 --> 00:21:12,740
Fifteen years passed.

262
00:21:14,670 --> 00:21:15,800
The war ended.

263
00:21:16,730 --> 00:21:22,640
And in the nineteen-fifties, oil companies
began to survey its deserts from the air.

264
00:21:24,490 --> 00:21:31,080
In 1958, crews working for those
companies spotted something on the sand,

265
00:21:31,510 --> 00:21:36,120
far out in the empty south-east:
the shape of a large aircraft.

266
00:21:37,410 --> 00:21:41,100
The accounts do not agree
on who saw it first.

267
00:21:42,350 --> 00:21:46,380
One names a British airline
crew in May of that year.

268
00:21:47,230 --> 00:21:53,040
Another names a survey plane flying for
D'Arcy, a British Petroleum company.

269
00:21:53,970 --> 00:21:57,580
The National Archives dates
the sighting to November.

270
00:21:59,110 --> 00:22:05,040
What they all agree on is this: the
wreck was real, it was American,

271
00:22:05,550 --> 00:22:09,800
and no one had reported a bomber
missing in that part of the desert.

272
00:22:11,180 --> 00:22:18,710
In February 1959, a small team of D'Arcy
geologists drove out across the sand to see

273
00:22:18,760 --> 00:22:20,250
the wreck for themselves.

274
00:22:21,900 --> 00:22:25,470
What they found was almost
impossible to believe.

275
00:22:26,440 --> 00:22:33,150
The B-24 had broken in two, behind
the wings, but much of it was intact.

276
00:22:34,280 --> 00:22:36,510
The machine guns were still there.

277
00:22:37,100 --> 00:22:41,050
The radio, by one account, still worked.

278
00:22:42,160 --> 00:22:44,470
There was food and water aboard.

279
00:22:45,540 --> 00:22:49,770
Wikipedia's account, drawing
on reports from the time,

280
00:22:49,940 --> 00:22:52,910
says a thermos of tea was still drinkable.

281
00:22:54,580 --> 00:22:58,730
In the navigator's compartment lay D.P.

282
00:22:59,260 --> 00:23:00,690
Hays' log.

283
00:23:01,100 --> 00:23:03,910
It recorded the flight out to Naples.

284
00:23:04,520 --> 00:23:08,390
For the long flight home, it said nothing.

285
00:23:10,160 --> 00:23:13,730
There was no one aboard, and no remains.

286
00:23:14,900 --> 00:23:20,370
Just the plane, sitting where it had slid
to a stop on the night of the fourth of

287
00:23:20,420 --> 00:23:21,730
April, 1943.

288
00:23:23,300 --> 00:23:28,290
The news reached Wheelus Air Base,
the American base near Tripoli.

289
00:23:30,500 --> 00:23:33,860
In 1959, the U.S.

290
00:23:34,600 --> 00:23:37,090
Air Force went looking for the crew.

291
00:23:38,560 --> 00:23:41,970
Teams from Wheelus searched
the area around the wreck,

292
00:23:42,520 --> 00:23:48,310
and the Seventeenth Air Force sent in
helicopters and a C-130 transport.

293
00:23:48,880 --> 00:23:55,770
In six days, by one count, they covered
about four hundred and fifty square miles.

294
00:23:57,720 --> 00:24:00,590
Then, in June, they found the trail.

295
00:24:02,220 --> 00:24:06,570
Flying boots, standing in the sand,
with the toes pointing north.

296
00:24:07,820 --> 00:24:11,770
Strips of parachute, weighted
with stones into arrowheads.

297
00:24:12,440 --> 00:24:18,750
The markers the men had left for exactly
this moment, sixteen years too late.

298
00:24:20,460 --> 00:24:25,970
But the trail ran into the dunes,
and in the dunes it disappeared.

299
00:24:26,960 --> 00:24:32,030
On the second of September,
1959, the search was called off.

300
00:24:32,800 --> 00:24:35,670
The crew had still not been found.

301
00:24:36,900 --> 00:24:42,290
In February 1960, it was oil
men again who found them.

302
00:24:43,480 --> 00:24:45,790
On the eleventh of February,

303
00:24:46,180 --> 00:24:51,010
a British Petroleum survey team came
across the remains of five men,

304
00:24:51,060 --> 00:24:53,690
lying close together on the gravel.

305
00:24:55,120 --> 00:24:56,210
Hatton.

306
00:24:57,380 --> 00:24:58,380
Toner.

307
00:24:59,393 --> 00:25:00,393
Hays.

308
00:25:01,167 --> 00:25:02,167
LaMotte.

309
00:25:02,940 --> 00:25:03,940
Adams.

310
00:25:04,420 --> 00:25:07,690
The five who had stayed behind to wait.

311
00:25:09,560 --> 00:25:13,050
Beside them was Robert Toner's diary.

312
00:25:15,440 --> 00:25:20,510
By Wikipedia's figure, they were about
eighty-one miles from the wreck of their

313
00:25:20,560 --> 00:25:21,560
plane.

314
00:25:22,320 --> 00:25:25,610
Every one of those miles, they had walked.

315
00:25:26,367 --> 00:25:32,310
[long pause] They had been waiting
there for almost seventeen years.

316
00:25:33,850 --> 00:25:35,740
And the three who went on?

317
00:25:37,430 --> 00:25:42,980
In May 1960, the Army and the
Air Force launched a new search,

318
00:25:43,210 --> 00:25:48,840
called Operation Climax, with a
C-130 and two small helicopters.

319
00:25:50,670 --> 00:25:56,180
On the twelfth of May, Guy Shelley
was found, far beyond the others.

320
00:25:57,210 --> 00:26:01,340
On the seventeenth, Harold
Ripslinger, further still.

321
00:26:02,870 --> 00:26:07,360
How far exactly, the records do not agree.

322
00:26:08,810 --> 00:26:13,900
Air and Space Forces Magazine puts Shelley
more than thirty-seven miles beyond the

323
00:26:13,950 --> 00:26:17,800
five, and Ripslinger
twenty-six miles beyond that.

324
00:26:18,610 --> 00:26:21,940
The National Archives
gives shorter figures.

325
00:26:22,970 --> 00:26:25,760
All of them describe the same thing:

326
00:26:26,150 --> 00:26:30,620
two men who kept walking long after
their bodies should have stopped.

327
00:26:32,450 --> 00:26:39,940
In August 1960, a BP team found John
Woravka, the man who never answered,

328
00:26:40,150 --> 00:26:42,280
about twelve miles from the crash.

329
00:26:43,470 --> 00:26:46,120
His parachute had not fully opened.

330
00:26:48,090 --> 00:26:50,020
Vernon Moore was never found.

331
00:26:55,270 --> 00:26:58,020
So what went wrong?

332
00:27:00,730 --> 00:27:06,320
The official verdict, as Air and
Space Forces Magazine puts it,

333
00:27:06,370 --> 00:27:09,040
was a gross navigational error.

334
00:27:10,670 --> 00:27:14,380
But that phrase hides a
chain of smaller things.

335
00:27:14,940 --> 00:27:17,960
A crew on its first combat mission.

336
00:27:19,090 --> 00:27:22,220
A dark night with no landmarks below.

337
00:27:22,570 --> 00:27:25,860
A direction finder that was not working.

338
00:27:26,230 --> 00:27:32,060
A radio bearing that could be read
two ways, and was read the wrong way.

339
00:27:32,250 --> 00:27:36,840
And a coastline that went
by underneath them, unseen.

340
00:27:38,630 --> 00:27:44,760
The National Archives points to the
same chain: inexperience, darkness,

341
00:27:44,930 --> 00:27:47,900
and an aircraft that simply flew too far.

342
00:27:49,390 --> 00:27:54,440
No single mistake killed the
crew of the Lady Be Good.

343
00:27:54,660 --> 00:27:59,520
A few ordinary ones, lined
up, in the dark, did.

344
00:28:00,780 --> 00:28:03,890
The story did not end with the search.

345
00:28:05,700 --> 00:28:11,650
Parts of the Lady Be Good were taken from
the wreck and fitted to other aircraft.

346
00:28:12,280 --> 00:28:16,510
A propeller was set up as a
memorial at Wheelus Air Base.

347
00:28:17,067 --> 00:28:22,250
A stained-glass window honoring the
crew was made for the base chapel,

348
00:28:22,300 --> 00:28:26,840
and later moved to the
National Museum of the U.S.

349
00:28:27,320 --> 00:28:28,790
Air Force.

350
00:28:30,860 --> 00:28:35,950
In 1994, what was left of the
plane was moved out of the desert,

351
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:40,950
and today it sits at an air base
near Tobruk, on the Libyan coast.

352
00:28:42,740 --> 00:28:44,830
The coast they were trying to reach.

353
00:28:45,780 --> 00:28:48,550
The bomber got there, in the end.

354
00:28:49,080 --> 00:28:51,090
Fifty-one years late.

355
00:28:52,420 --> 00:28:54,910
And in Lake Linden, Michigan,

356
00:28:55,360 --> 00:28:58,910
the small hometown of radio
operator Robert LaMotte,

357
00:28:59,240 --> 00:29:03,330
another of its propellers stands as
a memorial to the men who walked.

358
00:29:04,530 --> 00:29:08,940
Nine men flew out on the
fourth of April, 1943.

359
00:29:10,750 --> 00:29:13,640
One came down alone, in the dark.

360
00:29:14,690 --> 00:29:17,020
Five stopped together and waited.

361
00:29:17,890 --> 00:29:20,800
Two kept walking until they could not.

362
00:29:21,790 --> 00:29:26,960
And one, Vernon Moore, is still
out there, somewhere in the sand.

363
00:29:28,310 --> 00:29:31,440
The desert gave back almost everything.

364
00:29:32,070 --> 00:29:33,360
The plane.

365
00:29:34,250 --> 00:29:35,250
The guns.

366
00:29:36,270 --> 00:29:37,270
The log.

367
00:29:38,090 --> 00:29:39,090
The diary.

368
00:29:40,110 --> 00:29:42,660
It even gave back the tea.

369
00:29:44,370 --> 00:29:47,900
But it kept the one thing
that would have saved them:

370
00:29:48,110 --> 00:29:51,060
the simple knowledge of
where they really were.

371
00:29:52,030 --> 00:29:58,780
[long pause] If you could ask the crew
of the Lady Be Good one question,

372
00:29:59,530 --> 00:30:00,620
what would it be?

373
00:30:01,270 --> 00:30:03,180
Tell us in the comments.

374
00:30:05,270 --> 00:30:10,920
Next time on ICYFOX, we stay in
the skies of the Second World War,

375
00:30:11,250 --> 00:30:15,300
with the strange lights that
Allied pilots called foo fighters.
